== B3/B4: what K does each CODATA value need, and how sensitive is S to K? ==
  A - 1/(24A) = 137.0359997202 (miss vs T = 3.96e-9 = 24.8 sigma_CODATA) ; c = 1/(A^2 pi^2) = 5.3954663e-6
  dS/dK at K_fit = c/K^2 = 5.4687e-8 per unit K ; window of K within 1 sigma_CODATA (2.1e-8 abs): +-0.384 ; within the bar (6.85e-8 abs): +-1.25
  S(K = 10) = 137.03599918065 ; miss vs T = 2.66e-11 (0.167 sigma_CODATA)  <- no continued fraction at all (F = 0)
  S(K = 9.5) = 137.03599915225 ; miss vs T = 1.81e-10 (1.13 sigma_CODATA)
  S(K = 9) = 137.0359991207 ; miss vs T = 4.11e-10 (2.57 sigma_CODATA)
  (the five CODATA values are RECALLED from memory, not read from a source in this lane)
  2006 : K needed = 130.96609 (OUTSIDE the operator domain (9,10): the model cannot represent this value with any single continued fraction)
  2010 : K needed = 8.3495632 (OUTSIDE the operator domain (9,10): the model cannot represent this value with any single continued fraction)
  2014 : K needed = 9.283362245 (inside (9,10)) ; F = 10 - K has quotients [0; 1, 2, 1, 1, 8, 9, 2, 27]
  2018 : K needed = 8.4808049 (OUTSIDE the operator domain (9,10): the model cannot represent this value with any single continued fraction)
  2022 : K needed = 9.932790512 (inside (9,10)) ; F = 10 - K has quotients [0; 14, 1, 7, 3, 1, 13, 1, 1]
  CODATA values with K inside (9,10): 2 of 5
  Gauss-Kuzmin: P(a quotient > 45) = 0.0310 ; P(all 8 quotients <= 45) = 0.777 ; for the paper's claim over five values (40 quotients) = 0.283
